window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("strategy", {"topics":[{"key":"strategy/ai-roadmap","sources":[],"why":"Most organisations start with a list: twenty ideas collected in workshops, each championed by someone,\nnone compared on the same terms. The naive plan picks the most exciting ones, staffs them in parallel and\npromises dates. Three things then go wrong. First, the exciting ideas are often the ones with the least\nready data or the highest risk (customer-facing, regulated decisions), so they stall in pilot. Second,\nevery team rebuilds the same plumbing (model access, document ingestion, an eval set, logging), so the\nfifth project costs as much as the first. Third, the plan is pinned to a specific model or vendor, and\nwhen capability or pricing moves a few months later, half the plan is obsolete while the other half\nsolved problems that a new model now handles out of the box.\n\nA feature roadmap from ordinary software does not transfer cleanly either. In normal software, if you\nbuild the feature to spec, it works. In AI, you can build the system exactly as designed and still find\nit is only right some of the time, which is not enough for the workflow. Feasibility is an empirical\nquestion answered by an eval on real data, so the roadmap has to schedule *finding out* before it\nschedules *scaling*. Without that, the organisation discovers in month nine that a flagship use case\ncannot clear the accuracy bar, and the credibility loss poisons the next proposal.","how":"### 1. Build the candidate list from workflows, not technology\nEach candidate is a specific workflow step with an owner, a volume, a current cost or cycle time, and a\nuser who would act on the output (\"triage inbound claims emails into twelve queues\", not \"use GenAI in\nclaims\"). Frame it this way *because* value, baseline and adoption can only be measured on a workflow.\nUse-case discovery (see the scoping module) is where this list comes from; the roadmap is what you do\nwith it.\n\n### 2. Score on value and on feasibility separately\nValue is a small formula, not an adjective:\n\n```\nannual value ≈ volume × value per unit × capture rate × automation share − (run cost + review cost)\n```\n\nCapture rate is the part people skip: hours \"freed\" are worth nothing unless someone redeploys them or a\ncost line actually moves. Automation share is how much of the volume the system can handle at an\nacceptable error rate, which you do not know until the feasibility eval, so early value scores are\nranges. Run cost is inference at production volume, not pilot volume; a use case that is valuable at\n$0.02 per item can be uneconomic at $0.50. Feasibility has several independent axes, scored separately\n*because* a single gut-feel number hides which one will kill the project:\n- **Data readiness:** does the input data exist, can you legally use it, can you get access this quarter?\n- **Evaluability:** can you build a labelled set and define \"correct\"? If experts disagree on the answer,\n  you cannot prove the system works.\n- **Error tolerance:** what does a wrong output cost, and is there a human checkpoint? Drafting with review\n  tolerates errors that autonomous decisions do not.\n- **Risk class:** regulatory, privacy and reputational exposure (feeds the AI inventory and risk tiering).\n- **Integration effort:** read-only assistance is cheap; writing back into systems of record is not.\n\n### 3. Sequence for capability, not just for score\nOrder the portfolio so early projects are high-feasibility, moderate-value and *build reusable assets*:\nan access path to the document store, an evaluation harness, a gateway with logging, a review UI. Later,\nhigher-value but riskier projects then start with those assets in place. This is why the top-scoring\nidea is not always first: if it needs four foundations nobody has built, it goes second or third, behind\na project that builds two of them.\n\n### 4. Decide build, buy or wait for each item\nFor each candidate ask whether a vendor product already does it acceptably (buy), whether it needs your\ndata and workflow integration (build), or whether the blocker is raw model capability that the market is\nimproving quickly (wait). \"Wait\" is a legitimate roadmap state *because* building elaborate workarounds\nfor a capability gap is often wasted when the next model release closes it; but a waiting item needs a\ntrigger, such as \"re-run the feasibility eval whenever a new model in our approved set ships\", or it is\njust a polite way of dropping it.\n\n### 5. Attach a gate to every stage\nEach use case moves through discovery → feasibility eval → pilot → production → scale, and each\ntransition has a pre-agreed criterion and a kill condition. Example: \"proceed to pilot if the offline eval\non 300 labelled cases meets the agreed accuracy bar at the agreed cost per item; stop if after two\niterations it is still far below.\" Gates make cancelling cheap and normal, *because* the decision rule\nwas set before anyone was invested in the answer. The economics favour this: a feasibility eval costs\nweeks and a production build costs months, so the early gate is buying information cheaply before the\nexpensive commitment. Gates also change what they measure as the item matures: the feasibility gate\nchecks offline accuracy and cost per item; the pilot gate checks whether users actually act on the output\nand whether the workflow metric moved; the production gate checks cost at full volume, latency, monitoring\nand an owner on call.\n\n### 6. Plan in horizons and re-plan on a cadence\nNear term (this quarter): committed scope and dates. Mid term: ranked candidates with the foundations they\nneed. Long term: themes and bets, explicitly unscheduled. Review quarterly, and re-score whenever the\nmodel landscape moves: a capability jump can promote a parked use case; a price drop can flip one from\nuneconomic to obvious. Commit to *capabilities required* (\"extract fields from scanned forms at this\naccuracy\"), not to a named model, so the roadmap survives model churn.\n\n### 7. Make the portfolio visible\nKeep it as a living artefact: each row has owner, stage, gate criterion, latest eval result, cost per\nitem, and next decision date. This is also what governance needs for the AI inventory.","example":"A scoring pass over a small portfolio. Scores are 1–5 judgements from workshops; the numbers are\nillustrative, the mechanics are the point.\n\n```python\ncands = {  # value, data, evaluability, error_tolerance, needs (foundations)\n    \"claims_email_triage\": (3, 5, 5, 4, {\"gateway\", \"eval_harness\"}),\n    \"contract_clause_qa\":  (4, 4, 3, 3, {\"gateway\", \"eval_harness\", \"doc_ingest\"}),\n    \"auto_claim_decision\": (5, 2, 2, 1, {\"gateway\", \"eval_harness\", \"doc_ingest\", \"review_ui\"}),\n    \"agent_reply_drafts\":  (3, 4, 3, 4, {\"gateway\", \"review_ui\"}),\n}\ndef feasibility(d, e, t):\n    return min(d, e, t)          # the weakest axis is what kills a project, so use min, not mean\n\nbuilt, plan = set(), []\nremaining = dict(cands)\nwhile remaining:\n    def priority(item):\n        v, d, e, t, needs = item[1]\n        new_work = len(needs - built)            # foundations this project would have to build\n        return v * feasibility(d, e, t) - 2 * new_work\n    name, (v, d, e, t, needs) = max(remaining.items(), key=priority)\n    plan.append((name, v * feasibility(d, e, t), sorted(needs - built)))\n    built |= needs\n    del remaining[name]\n\nfor step, (name, score, builds) in enumerate(plan, 1):\n    print(step, name, \"score\", score, \"builds\", builds)\n# 1 claims_email_triage score 12 builds ['eval_harness', 'gateway']\n# 2 contract_clause_qa score 12 builds ['doc_ingest']\n# 3 agent_reply_drafts score 9 builds ['review_ui']\n# 4 auto_claim_decision score 5 builds []\n```\n\nThe highest-value item, automated claim decisions, comes last: its weakest axis (error tolerance 1) caps\nits feasibility, and by the time it starts every foundation it needs already exists. Contract Q&A\nbeats reply drafting for second place because, once the gateway and harness exist, each needs exactly one\nnew foundation (doc ingestion vs. review UI), so the tie-break is the base score: 12 against 9. Using `min` rather\nthan an average is deliberate: excellent data does not rescue a use case nobody can evaluate. The\npenalty of 2 points per new foundation is a judgement call, so check the plan is not an artefact of it:\nhere any weight from 1 to 4 gives the same order, which is what makes the sequence defensible in the\nmeeting. If a small change of weight reshuffles the plan, the scores are too close to decide on and the\nhonest move is a quick feasibility eval on the tied items.","tradeoffs":["Starting with modest, feasible use cases builds foundations and credibility, but delays the headline value; it flips when one high-value case is so dominant that funding depends on attacking it first.","Committing to capabilities instead of named models keeps the plan durable, but makes budgeting vaguer; once a use case is in production, pin the model and price it precisely.","Running many small parallel pilots surfaces feasibility fast, but spreads scarce expert labellers and platform engineers thin; with a small team, run two or three sequentially with hard gates.","Building shared foundations early lowers marginal cost later, but risks a platform with no users; build each foundation only when a scheduled use case needs it."],"pitfalls":["Ranking by excitement or executive sponsorship alone; the symptom is a pilot graveyard where several projects sit for months at \"almost good enough\".","No kill criteria at gates; projects never stop, they just quietly lose staff, and the portfolio view shows many items \"in progress\" with no recent eval result.","Counting freed hours as value without a plan to redeploy them; finance later finds no cost moved and the next roadmap gets less funding.","Pinning the plan to one model's capabilities; when a new release changes what is easy, the team keeps building workarounds that the new model makes unnecessary.","Skipping baseline measurement before deployment; the symptom is a launch review where the system looks good but nobody can say by how much it beats the old process, so the next item gets no funding.","Treating a pilot pass as production readiness; costs and error rates jump at full volume or on input types the hand-picked pilot users never sent, and the item slides back to \"almost done\"."],"when":"Write a roadmap as soon as there are more candidate use cases than people to build them, which is almost\nimmediately. Keep it short-horizon and evidence-driven: the quarter's commitments in detail, the rest as\nranked hypotheses. Measure each item with its gate metric (offline eval score, cost per item, adoption in\npilot) and review quarterly or when the model market moves. Measure the portfolio too: time from idea to\nfeasibility verdict, the share of items with a gate decision in the last quarter, and the kill rate. A\nkill rate of zero is not a sign of good ideas; it means the gates are not being applied. Do not produce a three-year plan of dated AI\nfeatures; it will be wrong within months and will make every later change look like failure. In\ninterviews, a strong answer separates value from feasibility, names evaluability and data access as the\nreal constraints, and explains how sequencing builds reusable capability.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns a roadmap item into a feasibility eval on real data quickly, and reports results in the gate's terms so the go/stop call is obvious.","architect":"Owns sequencing and the foundation list: which shared capabilities each stage builds, and which risk tier each use case sits in.","fde":"Runs discovery inside the customer, scores their candidates honestly, and steers them away from the flagship-but-infeasible use case toward a first win that unlocks the rest."},"check":{"q":"An executive wants the roadmap to lead with fully automated loan decisions because it has the\nlargest projected savings. How do you respond?","a":"I would not refuse it, I would sequence it. Automated credit decisions score high on value but low\non error tolerance and high on regulatory risk, and they need foundations we lack: document ingestion, an\neval set agreed by credit experts, an audit trail and a review UI. I would propose a first stage that\nbuilds those through a lower-risk adjacent use case, such as extracting and summarising applications for\nunderwriters, with a gate: if extraction accuracy on a few hundred labelled files meets the bar, proceed\nto decision support with a human approving, and only consider autonomy once the error profile is measured\nin production. The savings case stays on the roadmap, with evidence gates instead of a date."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why score feasibility as the minimum of its axes rather than their average?","options":["Averages are harder to compute consistently across workshops","A single blocking constraint, such as no way to evaluate correctness, stops a project regardless of strengths elsewhere","The minimum always produces a higher priority for high-value projects","Averaging would double-count the value score"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Feasibility constraints are conjunctive: excellent data cannot compensate for an output nobody can verify. An average lets one strong axis mask the one that will actually stall the project in pilot."},{"q":"What is the main reason an AI roadmap should commit to required capabilities rather than named models?","options":["Named models are not allowed in governance documents","Capabilities are easier to explain to executives","Model capability and pricing change faster than the planning cycle, so model-pinned plans go stale","Vendors do not allow customers to plan around specific model versions"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Over a planning horizon, new releases change what is easy and what it costs. Stating the capability and its bar lets any model that clears it be used, while production items still pin a specific model."},{"q":"A use case has the highest value score but needs four foundations nobody has built. What does good sequencing usually do?","options":["Start it first because value dominates every other factor","Drop it from the roadmap as infeasible","Schedule it after projects that build those foundations, so it starts with them in place","Build all four foundations as a standalone platform project before any use case"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Sequencing for capability lets cheaper, feasible projects build the shared assets while producing value. Building foundations with no consuming use case risks an unused platform; dropping it discards the value."}]},{"key":"strategy/platform-strategy","sources":[],"why":"Once a company has more than a couple of AI projects, the same needs recur: an API key to a model\nprovider, a way to log prompts and costs, a document-ingestion pipeline, an eval set and harness, PII\nredaction, a review UI. Left alone, each team builds its own. That has visible costs (duplicated\nengineering, several contracts with the same provider at worse prices) and invisible ones: no single\ninventory of where models are used, no consistent data-retention policy, no way to rotate a key or swap a\ndeprecated model everywhere, and no organisation-wide view of spend.\n\nThe naive fix is the opposite extreme: a central team designs a comprehensive \"AI platform\" up front and\nmandates it. That fails in a different way. The platform is designed from guesses about requirements, so\nit fits nobody exactly; it is built before anyone uses it, so there is no feedback; and because it is the\nonly sanctioned path, every missing feature becomes a ticket in a queue while product teams wait. Teams\nthen route around it, and you have both a platform and shadow stacks.\n\nPlatform strategy is the discipline between these: deciding the boundary, the timing, the operating\nmodel and the funding so the shared layer is pulled by demand rather than pushed by architecture. It\nconnects to two neighbouring lessons: build-vs-buy decides how each layer is sourced, and\ncentralised-vs-federated decides the organisational ownership model. This lesson is about the sequencing\nand the economics that tie them together.","how":"### 1. Inventory the recurring capabilities\nList what each current and roadmapped use case needs, layer by layer: model access, routing and\nfallback, secrets, prompt and model registry, retrieval and ingestion, evaluation, observability and cost\nattribution, guardrails, human review. The inventory is the demand signal; without it, the platform is\ndesigned from imagination.\n\n### 2. Score each capability on three questions\n- **Breadth of demand:** how many teams need it now or in the next two quarters? A common heuristic is to\n  wait until the third consumer, *because* by then you have seen enough variation to generalise correctly.\n- **Variance between teams:** do they need roughly the same thing (a gateway, audit logging) or genuinely\n  different things (a retrieval strategy tuned to legal contracts vs. support tickets)? Low-variance\n  capabilities centralise well; high-variance ones become a lowest-common-denominator abstraction that\n  every team fights.\n- **Externalities:** does getting it wrong in one team create risk or cost for everyone (leaked keys,\n  data sent to an unapproved region, uncapped spend, no audit trail)? Those belong in the shared layer\n  even with low demand, *because* the cost of inconsistency is organisational.\n\n### 3. Check the economics before you centralise\nThe three questions combine into a rough break-even. With *n* teams each spending *c_team* to build and\nrun their own version, and a platform costing *C_platform* to build and run plus *c_adopt* per team to\nintegrate and adapt:\n\n```\ncentralise when  n × c_team  >  C_platform + n × c_adopt + coordination cost\n                 (+ the expected cost of an inconsistency incident, for high-externality items)\n```\n\nVariance is what makes this fail: when teams' needs differ, *c_adopt* rises toward *c_team* (every team\nstill does most of the work, now fighting an abstraction), and the inequality never holds however large\n*n* gets. Externalities enter as the last term, which is why a gateway or audit log can justify itself at\n*n* = 2 while a shared chunking library cannot at *n* = 6. The same arithmetic covers sourcing: if a\ncommercial or open-source gateway gives you most of *C_platform* for less, the platform team's job is to\noperate and integrate it, not to write one.\n\n### 4. Draw a thin mandatory core and an optional paved road\nThe core is what every AI workload must go through: identity, approved model access through a gateway,\nbudgets and rate limits, logging to a common trace store, data-classification rules. It is mandatory\n*because* its value comes from being universal (one inventory, one place to swap a model). The paved road\nis everything else the platform offers as defaults (an eval harness, an ingestion template, a review UI):\noptional, but easier than building your own. Teams that leave the paved road accept owning what they\nbuild.\n\n### 5. Treat the platform as a product\nIt has users (internal teams), a roadmap driven by their needs, documentation, SLOs and adoption metrics:\nnumber of teams onboarded, time from idea to first production call, share of spend flowing through the\ngateway. A platform team measured on features shipped rather than on consumer outcomes drifts toward the\nover-built monolith described above.\n\n### 6. Choose a funding model\nCentral funding makes adoption easy but hides cost. Showback (reporting each team's usage) creates\nawareness without friction. Chargeback (billing teams) aligns incentives but can push teams to route\naround the platform to save money. Many organisations start with central funding plus showback and move\nto chargeback for variable costs such as tokens once usage is significant.\n\n### 7. Keep seams so decisions are reversible\nExpose the platform through stable internal interfaces (an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, a trace schema,\nan eval-result format) rather than leaking a vendor's SDK into every service, *because* the model and\ntooling market changes quickly and you will swap components under the interface.\n\n### 8. Engineer the core as shared infrastructure\nA mandatory gateway is on every AI request path, so its failure is everyone's outage. Run it with the\navailability target of the most demanding consumer, keep it stateless and horizontally scaled, and decide\nup front what happens when it is down (fail closed for high-risk traffic, fail open to a direct route only\nwhere policy allows), *because* the centralisation that gives you one place to swap a model also gives you\none place to break all of them.","example":"A worked scenario. An insurer has six product teams using LLMs. The platform lead tabulates the\ncapabilities (team counts come from the inventory; the scenario is illustrative):\n\n| Capability | Teams needing it | Variance | Externality | Decision |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Model access + keys + budgets | 6 | low | high (spend, data region) | mandatory core |\n| Prompt/response logging, cost attribution | 6 | low | high (audit) | mandatory core |\n| PII redaction before the model | 4 | low | high (privacy) | mandatory core |\n| Eval harness + result store | 5 | medium | medium | paved road |\n| Document ingestion pipeline | 3 | medium | low | paved road, template |\n| Retrieval strategy / chunking | 3 | high | low | team-owned |\n| Agent framework | 2 | high | low | team-owned, revisit |\n\nBefore the platform, each team had its own provider account; finance saw six invoices and no per-feature\nbreakdown, and security found two teams sending claim documents to a model region the data policy did\nnot allow. The first platform release is deliberately small: a gateway in front of approved models, with\nper-team budgets, common logging and redaction. Migration is cheap for teams because the gateway speaks\nthe same API shape they already call. Within a quarter, all spend is attributable per feature, one team's\nmodel deprecation is handled by changing a routing rule once that team's evals pass on the\nreplacement model, and the eval harness is offered next because\nfive teams asked for it. Agent frameworks stay team-owned: only two teams use them and their needs\ndiffer, so a shared abstraction now would freeze the wrong design.","tradeoffs":["A mandatory core gives one inventory, one audit trail and one place to swap models, but adds a hop and a dependency; it flips toward federation when latency budgets are tight or teams are in separate regulatory domains.","Building the platform after the third consumer generalises from real needs, but tolerates some early duplication; it flips when an externality (data residency, spend) makes even two divergent stacks unacceptable.","Abstracting over model providers enables switching and routing, but hides provider-specific features (caching modes, tool formats); allow pass-through for teams that need those features.","Chargeback aligns usage with cost, but taxes adoption; prefer showback until the platform is the default path and token spend is material."],"pitfalls":["Building a comprehensive platform before any consumer exists; the symptom is a polished system with low adoption and teams quietly keeping their own API keys.","Centralising high-variance capabilities like retrieval strategy; teams file endless feature requests or fork the shared component, and its configuration surface balloons.","A platform team measured on output, not adoption; it ships features nobody asked for while the top consumer request ages in the backlog.","Leaking one vendor's SDK through the platform interface; the first provider migration turns into a change in every consuming service.","No exit path for teams off the paved road; exceptions accumulate as unreviewed shadow stacks that show up as provider invoices finance cannot map to any registered workload.","Running the gateway like an internal tool rather than tier-one infrastructure; the symptom is a single gateway incident that takes down every AI feature at once, followed by teams asking for bypass routes."],"when":"Make platform decisions explicitly once two or three teams are building on models, or earlier if a\ncompliance or spend externality already exists. Start with the thin core: gateway, identity, budgets,\nlogging. Add paved-road components when demand is proven and variance is low. Measure adoption, time to\nfirst production call, share of traffic and spend through the core, lead time on the platform's top\nconsumer requests, and the number of approved exceptions (a rising count means the paved road no longer\nfits). Do not build a\nplatform as the first AI project; build it out of the first projects' shared parts. In interviews, show\nyou can name what to centralise and why, and when you would deliberately leave duplication in place.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Consumes the platform through its stable interface and contributes back components that a second team clearly needs, rather than forking or bypassing it.","architect":"Owns the boundary between mandatory core, paved road and team-owned layers, and the interfaces that keep vendor choices reversible.","fde":"Notices which customer solutions repeat across accounts and argues, with the evidence, for turning them into platform or product capabilities."},"check":{"q":"Two teams want a shared agent framework built by the platform team; four other teams use plain\nmodel calls. Should the platform build it now?","a":"Probably not yet. Two consumers is thin demand, and agent frameworks are high-variance: tool\ncontracts, state handling and orchestration differ a lot between use cases and the ecosystem is still\nmoving. A shared abstraction now would likely encode one team's design and become a constraint for the\nother. What the platform should provide now are the low-variance parts both teams need anyway: model\naccess through the gateway, tracing, budgets, sandboxed tool credentials. Revisit when a third team needs\nagents and the common shape is visible, and keep a light standard (trace format, approval-gate pattern)\nso convergence later is cheap."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which capability is the strongest candidate for the mandatory platform core?","options":["A chunking strategy tuned for one team's contracts","An agent framework used by two teams","Approved model access with per-team budgets and audit logging","A shared prompt library for marketing copy"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Model access, budgets and audit are needed by every team, vary little, and create organisation-wide risk when done inconsistently. The chunking strategy and agent framework are high-variance and fit poorly."},{"q":"Why is \"wait for the third consumer\" a common heuristic before platformising a capability?","options":["Platform teams are usually staffed only after three projects exist","By then the real variation in needs is visible, so the shared design generalises from evidence","Licensing for shared tools typically requires three internal customers","Two teams can always share code without a platform"],"answer":1,"explanation":"One or two consumers give too little signal to separate essential needs from incidental ones. The heuristic is overridden when an externality such as data residency makes divergence unacceptable early."},{"q":"What is the usual effect of switching to chargeback before the platform is the default path?","options":["Adoption rises because teams can see their costs","Platform costs fall because usage becomes more efficient","Some teams route around the platform to avoid the bill, recreating shadow stacks","It has no effect, because the costs are the same either way"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Billing teams for a platform they could bypass makes bypassing look cheaper locally. Showback first builds cost awareness without that incentive, and chargeback works once the core is unavoidable."}]},{"key":"strategy/vendor-evaluation","sources":[],"why":"AI vendors are unusually hard to compare. Public benchmarks measure tasks that may look nothing like\nyours, and some benchmark data has leaked into training sets, so a leaderboard gap may not transfer.\nDemos are run on the vendor's chosen examples. Pricing is per token, per query, per seat or per\n\"credit\", which makes headline prices incomparable until you convert them to cost per unit of your work.\nAnd the decisive properties are often non-functional: whether your data is retained or used for training,\nwhich regions process it, what rate limits you get, how much notice you get before a model is deprecated,\nand how hard it is to leave.\n\nThe naive process, a feature checklist plus a demo plus the lowest list price, fails predictably. The\nchecklist rewards breadth over fit. The demo hides the failure modes on your messy inputs. The list price\nignores that a cheaper model needing two retries, longer prompts or more human review can cost more per\ncorrect outcome. Teams that choose this way discover the gaps after integration, when switching is\nexpensive, and then live with a vendor that fails a security review or throttles them at peak.\n\nEvaluation also recurs: model vendors ship new versions frequently, so the same harness should be\nre-runnable when a new candidate appears, rather than a one-off procurement exercise.","how":"### 1. Write requirements as gates and weighted criteria\nSeparate **must-haves** (pass/fail) from **differentiators** (scored). Gates typically cover data use\n(no training on your data, retention period, zero-retention options), data residency, security\nattestations and the answers your security questionnaire needs, required deployment mode (API, VPC,\nself-hosted), and compliance obligations. A vendor that fails a gate is out regardless of score,\n*because* a weighted average lets a high quality score compensate for a disqualifying flaw. Fix the\nweights for the differentiators **before** seeing results, so the scoring is not reverse-engineered to\njustify a favourite.\n\n### 2. Build a task-specific evaluation set\nUse a few hundred real, representative, labelled examples from your workflow, including the hard and\nugly cases (scanned PDFs, long threads, other languages). Keep it private and never send it to vendors\nfor \"tuning\" before the test, *because* then you are measuring their preparation, not their product.\n\nSize the set for the differences you need to detect. The 95% interval on an accuracy *p* measured on *n*\nitems is roughly ±1.96 × √(p(1−p)/n): at p = 0.95 and n = 400 that is about ±2 points. So two vendors 1\npoint apart on 400 items are tied, and the decision must rest on cost, latency or terms. Because every\nvendor runs on the same items, compare them *paired*: count the items A gets right and B gets wrong, and\nvice versa. Only the disagreements carry information, which makes paired comparison more sensitive than\ncomparing two headline percentages, and reading those disagreements tells you *what kind* of input each\nvendor fails on.\n\n### 3. Run a controlled bake-off\nSame harness, same scoring, and an equal prompt-engineering budget per vendor, tuned on a separate\ndevelopment split and scored once on the held-out test split. Reusing prompts tuned for the incumbent\nquietly favours it, *because* those prompts encode that model's quirks. If outputs are sampled, run each\nitem more than once or fix the decoding settings, and report the spread. For each candidate measure:\n- **Quality** on your metric (accuracy, faithfulness, pass rate on acceptance criteria).\n- **Latency** at p50 and p95 at your expected concurrency, not single requests, *because* rate limits and\n  queueing appear only under load.\n- **Cost per successful task:** total cost (tokens, retries, fixed fees, human review of failures)\n  divided by tasks completed correctly. Count each cost once: if a human fixes a failure, that task is\n  delivered and its review cost is in the numerator; if failures are simply discarded, there is no review\n  cost but they drop out of the denominator. This is the number that makes different pricing models\n  comparable.\n- **Operational fit:** quotas in your region, SLA terms, observability hooks, SDK quality, support.\n\nThe same structure applies to non-model vendors with different metrics: a vector database is measured on\nrecall@k and p95 query latency at your corpus size and with your metadata filters, plus ingestion and\nupdate throughput, *because* index behaviour changes with scale and filter selectivity in ways a small\ndemo corpus never shows.\n\n### 4. Assess the vendor, not just the product\nFinancial and roadmap stability, model deprecation policy and notice period, how they communicate\nchanges, and whether a model version can be pinned. For a small vendor, ask what happens to your data\nand service if they are acquired or shut down.\n\n### 5. Price the exit before you enter\nEstimate the cost of leaving: proprietary formats, embeddings that must be regenerated, prompts tuned to\none model, contractual minimums. Prefer vendors that fit behind your own interface (gateway, standard\nAPIs), *because* a low exit cost is what keeps the next negotiation honest.\n\n### 6. Decide, document, and schedule re-evaluation\nRecord the scores, the gates and the reasoning in a decision record, and re-run the harness when a\nmaterial new option or price change appears.","example":"Scoring three model providers for invoice field extraction. Quality and latency come from a bake-off on\n400 labelled invoices; prices and figures are illustrative.\n\n```python\nvendors = {\n    #       gates_ok, accuracy, p95_s, cost_per_call, review_cost_per_failure\n    \"A\": (True,  0.96, 2.8, 0.010, 0.50),\n    \"B\": (True,  0.91, 1.2, 0.002, 0.50),\n    \"C\": (False, 0.97, 1.9, 0.006, 0.50),   # fails residency gate: data leaves the region\n}\nweights = {\"cost\": 0.5, \"latency\": 0.2, \"accuracy\": 0.3}   # fixed before the bake-off\nP95_BUDGET = 3.0\n\ndef cost_per_success(acc, call, review):\n    # every invoice ends correct: the model's output, or a human fix of a flagged failure\n    return call + (1 - acc) * review   # each pays one call; failures also pay a review\n\nrows = {}\nfor v, (ok, acc, p95, call, review) in vendors.items():\n    if not ok or p95 > P95_BUDGET:\n        print(v, \"excluded by gate\"); continue\n    rows[v] = (acc, p95, cost_per_success(acc, call, review))\n\nbest_cost = min(r[2] for r in rows.values()); best_p95 = min(r[1] for r in rows.values())\nfor v, (acc, p95, cps) in rows.items():\n    score = (weights[\"cost\"] * best_cost / cps + weights[\"latency\"] * best_p95 / p95\n             + weights[\"accuracy\"] * acc)\n    print(f\"{v}: acc={acc:.2f} p95={p95}s cost/success=${cps:.4f} score={score:.3f}\")\n# C excluded by gate\n# A: acc=0.96 p95=2.8s cost/success=$0.0300 score=0.874\n# B: acc=0.91 p95=1.2s cost/success=$0.0470 score=0.792\n```\n\nB's call price is five times lower, but its extra 5 points of errors each need a 50-cent human fix, so\nits cost per successful extraction is higher than A's ($0.047 vs $0.030). C has the best accuracy and is\nout anyway, and its one-point lead over A is inside the ±2-point noise on 400 items, so it was not a\nclear winner even before the gate. A's 5-point lead over B is outside it. The model assumes failures are\ncaught (by validation rules or review); if they slip through undetected, their downstream cost belongs in\nthe review term and usually dwarfs the call price. Note that accuracy enters twice, inside cost per\nsuccess and as its own weighted term; that is defensible only if the accuracy weight stands for something\ncost does not capture (undetected errors, user trust). Otherwise drop it, or the scoring double-counts.","tradeoffs":["A rigorous bake-off on your data gives decisions that transfer to production, but costs weeks of labelling and engineering; for low-risk, easily reversible choices a lighter pilot is enough.","Multi-vendor setups behind a gateway reduce lock-in and outage risk, but multiply security reviews, contracts and prompt maintenance; they pay off when volume or criticality is high.","Choosing the top-quality vendor maximises accuracy, but may cost more per call; it flips when error costs are low and a cheaper model's failures are cheap to catch.","Long-term commitments buy discounts, but raise exit cost in a market where prices often fall and better models appear; keep terms short when the category is moving fast."],"pitfalls":["Trusting public leaderboards for a domain task; the symptom is a vendor that ranked first but loses on your own invoices or tickets.","Comparing list prices instead of cost per successful task; the cheap option turns out expensive once retries and human correction are counted.","Testing latency with single requests; production hits rate limits or queueing at peak and p95 latency breaks the user-facing budget.","Setting weights after seeing results; the decision record reads like a justification, and the next audit or new stakeholder challenges it.","Ignoring deprecation policy; a pinned model is retired with short notice and the team must re-validate prompts and evals in a hurry.","Running challengers on prompts tuned for the incumbent; the incumbent wins every bake-off, and the gap shrinks or vanishes once someone spends a few days prompting the challenger properly.","Declaring a winner on a one- or two-point gap from a few hundred items; the \"better\" vendor's lead evaporates on the next re-run or the production sample."],"when":"Run a structured evaluation whenever the choice is expensive to reverse or touches sensitive data:\nprimary model provider, vector store, platform vendors, anything inside the customer's data boundary.\nUse a light version (gates plus a small bake-off) for tools that are cheap to swap. Measure quality on\nyour own set with its confidence interval, p95 latency at load, cost per successful task, and exit cost. Re-run when new models ship\nor prices change. In interviews, the signal is separating gates from scores, insisting on your own data,\nand converting pricing to cost per correct outcome.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the reusable bake-off harness, runs candidates on the same labelled set, and reports quality, p95 latency and cost per success in one table.","architect":"Defines the gates and weights, owns the decision record, and makes sure the chosen vendor sits behind an interface that keeps exit cost low.","fde":"Evaluates vendors against the customer's own constraints (their region, procurement rules, security questionnaire) and data, not the vendor's demo set."},"check":{"q":"Vendor X's model is cheaper per token than Vendor Y's and scores slightly lower on your eval.\nProcurement wants X. What do you check before agreeing?","a":"First, that X passes every gate: data use and retention terms, residency, security answers,\ndeployment mode. Next, whether \"slightly lower\" is real: on a few hundred items a one- or two-point gap\nmay be noise, so I would look at the paired disagreements and what kind of inputs X gets wrong. Then convert both to cost per successful task: X may need longer prompts, more retries or\nmore human correction, and if failures cost real review time, a small accuracy gap can outweigh a\nlarge token discount. I would also check p95 latency and rate limits at our concurrency, the deprecation\npolicy and version pinning, and the exit cost. If X still wins on cost per success within the latency\nbudget, X is the right call; if not, the table makes the case to procurement in their own terms."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are must-have requirements handled as gates instead of heavily weighted criteria?","options":["Gates are faster to evaluate than weighted scores","A weighted average lets strong scores elsewhere compensate for a disqualifying flaw","Vendors prefer pass/fail requirements in procurement","Weighted scoring cannot handle more than five criteria"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A vendor that violates residency or trains on your data is unacceptable whatever its accuracy. Any finite weight lets enough quality offset the violation, so the requirement must be a hard filter."},{"q":"Which metric best compares vendors that use different pricing models?","options":["Price per million input tokens","Monthly platform fee","Total cost divided by the number of tasks completed correctly","Rank on a public benchmark divided by price"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cost per successful task folds in token prices, retries, fixed fees and the cost of handling failures, so a per-token discount and a per-seat plan become directly comparable on the outcome you pay for."},{"q":"Why should latency in a bake-off be measured at expected concurrency?","options":["Single-request latency is always higher than under load","Vendors charge less for concurrent requests","Rate limits and queueing only appear under load, and they drive p95 latency","Concurrency improves accuracy through batching"],"answer":2,"explanation":"One request at a time hides throttling and queueing delays. The user-facing latency budget is usually a tail percentile, so it must be measured under production-like concurrency."}]},{"key":"strategy/technical-due-diligence","sources":[],"why":"Deals are priced on claims: \"proprietary model\", \"95% accuracy\", \"data moat\", \"70% gross margin\",\n\"enterprise-ready\". In conventional software, diligence checks code quality, architecture, security and\nteam. Those still matter, but AI adds failure modes that a code review cannot see. Accuracy can be an\nartefact of a test set the target curated or trained on. A \"proprietary model\" may be prompts around a\nthird-party API whose terms or prices can change. Training data may have been collected without the\nrights the business model assumes. Inference costs scale with usage, so margins that look healthy at\ncurrent volume can compress as customers use the product more, or as the product adds longer context or\nagentic loops.\n\nThe naive approach is to review slides, a demo and the architecture diagram, and to ask the founders.\nThat checks presentation, not reality: a demo runs on chosen inputs, a diagram shows intent rather than\nwhat is deployed, and the people answering have every reason to be optimistic. The cost of missing a\nproblem is asymmetric: discovering after closing that accuracy does not hold on the acquirer's customers,\nor that a key dataset cannot be used commercially, can erase the deal's value. Diligence exists to turn\nclaims into evidence before the price is fixed.","how":"### 1. Start from the thesis, not a checklist\nWrite down the claims the deal depends on (\"their extraction is materially better than what we could\nbuild in a year\", \"margins hold at 10× volume\", \"we can deploy it in our regulated customers' VPCs\").\nEach claim becomes a hypothesis with the evidence that would confirm or refute it. This focuses limited\ntime *because* a generic checklist spends equal effort on things that do not affect value.\n\n### 2. Verify the AI actually works, independently\nRequest their eval methodology, datasets and recent results, then go further: run the system on a\nheld-out sample **you** provide, from your own or your customers' data, scored by your criteria. Ask how\nthe eval set was built and whether any of it was used in training or prompt tuning (contamination). A\ngap between their number and yours is the most informative finding in AI diligence.\n\nThen explain the gap, *because* its cause decides the remedy. Break both results down by input segment\n(document type, language, scan quality, customer). If the target is equally good per segment and only the\nmix differs, its overall number simply reflects its own customer base, and you can predict yours by\nre-weighting the per-segment accuracy by your mix. If it is worse on the same segments, the reported\nnumber was inflated by curation or tuning on the eval set. Deals rarely allow free data flow, so plan the\nmechanics early: the target runs its system on your sample under NDA while you hold the labels and do\nthe scoring, or the test runs in a clean room with a small \"clean team\" who do not share raw data with the\nwider deal team.\n\n### 3. Map dependencies and their terms\nList every model, API, dataset, and open-source component in the production path, with license and\ncontract terms: is a third-party model's output allowed in this use, can the provider change pricing or\ndeprecate the model, do open-weight model licenses permit the intended commercial scale, is the data\nprocessed in regions customers accept. Ask what share of the product would survive the main provider\ndoubling prices or withdrawing a model.\n\n### 4. Establish data rights and provenance\nFor training, fine-tuning and retrieval data: where it came from, under what consent or license, whether\ncustomer data is used across customers, and whether deletion requests can be honoured, including in\ntrained weights. Missing provenance is a legal and reputational liability that transfers with the deal.\n\n### 5. Rebuild the unit economics\nFrom billing data and traces, compute cost per request and per customer, then project at higher usage:\ntoken growth per task, retries, agent steps, human review. Check whether the target's pricing model passes\nthose costs on. Margins that depend on a provider's current discount are a risk, not a fact.\n\n### 6. Check security of the AI layer\nBeyond the usual application review, ask the questions specific to AI systems: is retrieval scoped per\ntenant, so one customer's documents can never appear in another's context, including through shared\ncaches or indexes? What can an agentic feature do with its tool credentials if a document contains a\nprompt injection? Where do prompts and outputs get logged, and for how long? Where do model weights and\ndatasets come from, and are third-party checkpoints loaded in formats that can execute code? These\nmatter *because* a cross-tenant leak in an acquired product becomes the acquirer's incident.\n\n### 7. Check operability and the team\nIs there monitoring, an incident history, an eval-gated release process, reproducible training? Can the\nsystem be rebuilt from the repository? How concentrated is knowledge (key-person risk)? Interview\nengineers separately from leadership, *because* the most useful information is often the mismatch.\n\n### 8. Classify findings by consequence\nEach finding is a deal-breaker, a price or terms adjustment (escrow, warranty, earn-out tied to a\nmetric), or a post-close remediation item with an estimated cost. This converts technical findings into\ndecisions the deal team can act on.","example":"A worked scenario. A company considers acquiring a startup that sells AI contract review. Claims: \"94%\nclause-extraction accuracy\" and \"68% gross margin\". All figures are illustrative.\n\n**Accuracy.** The startup's eval set has 1,000 contracts it selected; the diligence team learns 300 of\nthem were also used to iterate on prompts. The team supplies 200 contracts from its own customers. Result:\n86% on the acquirer's sample. With 200 contracts the 95% interval on 86% is about ±5 points, so the gap to\n94% is real, not sampling noise. Segmenting explains most of it: on the 128 clean, digital English\ncontracts the system scores about 95%, on the 72 scanned or non-English ones about 70% (an interval of\nroughly ±10 points at that size). The acquirer's mix is 64/36, so 0.64 × 95% + 0.36 × 70% ≈ 86%. The\nstartup's own set is about 95% clean documents, and 0.95 × 95% + 0.05 × 70% ≈ 94%. So the headline is\nmostly a mix effect: the product is genuinely strong on clean contracts and weak on the inputs the\nacquirer's customers send more often. The prompt tuning on 300 eval contracts still means the 94% should\nbe treated as optimistic, but the actionable finding is the OCR and multilingual gap, which has a cost to\nfix that can be estimated.\n\n**Economics.** Revenue per customer averages $4,000 per month. Current inference cost is $900 per month per\ncustomer, plus $400 of hosting and support, giving (4,000 − 1,300) / 4,000 ≈ 68%. But the roadmap adds a\nmulti-step agent review that the startup's own traces show uses roughly 3× the tokens per contract.\nAt the same price and token rates, inference becomes $2,700 and margin falls to (4,000 − 3,100) / 4,000 =\n22.5%. Margin also depends on a discounted rate with one provider that expires next year.\n\n**Dependencies.** Everything runs on one external model API; no abstraction layer; prompts tuned\nspecifically to that model.\n\n**Classification.** Not a deal-breaker. Price adjustment for the margin risk; an earn-out tied to accuracy\non the acquirer's own held-out set; post-close remediation budget for OCR improvements and a gateway\nabstraction, sized from the 70% segment. Without independent testing, the deal would have been priced on\n94% and 68%.","tradeoffs":["Independent re-testing on your own data gives the most reliable evidence, but requires data-sharing agreements and time; when the timeline is short, a smaller sample still beats taking the claim on trust.","Deep code and infrastructure review finds operability risk, but is expensive; scale it to how much of the deal value depends on running the system yourself rather than on its customers or team.","Treating heavy reliance on a third-party model as a red flag protects against provider risk, but can undervalue a target whose real asset is workflow, data and distribution; judge the cost of switching, not the dependency itself.","Strict findings protect the buyer, but can kill reasonable deals; classifying into deal-breaker, adjustment and remediation keeps the response proportionate."],"pitfalls":["Accepting the target's accuracy number on its own eval set; the symptom appears after closing, when quality drops on the acquirer's customers and nobody can explain why.","Ignoring train/test overlap or prompt tuning on the eval set; scores look remarkably stable and high but fall sharply on any genuinely new sample.","Computing gross margin at today's usage and today's discounted prices; margin erodes as features add tokens and the discount lapses.","Skipping data-provenance questions; a later customer audit or deletion request reveals the target cannot show the rights to its training data.","Leaving the AI layer out of the security review; a post-close penetration test finds one customer's documents retrievable by another through a shared index or response cache.","Interviewing only founders; key-person risk and undocumented systems surface only when that person leaves after the earn-out period."],"when":"Run AI-specific technical diligence for any acquisition, investment or strategic partnership where AI is\npart of the value, and in lighter form for a critical vendor whose failure would hurt you (see vendor\nevaluation, which covers the comparative selection process). Scale depth to deal size and to how much\nvalue depends on the AI claims. The outputs to aim for are an independent accuracy number on your data\nbroken down by segment, a dependency and license map, an AI-layer security summary, a data-rights summary, a usage-sensitive margin model, and a classified\nfindings list. In interviews, the strong signal is turning each headline claim into a test you would run.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the independent eval on held-out data, reads traces to rebuild cost per request, and checks whether the system can actually be rebuilt and deployed from the repository.","architect":"Owns the dependency map, provider-exposure analysis and integration risk, and estimates the remediation needed to make the target fit the acquirer's platform and controls.","fde":"Brings real customer data and environments to the test, and judges whether the product will survive deployment in regulated or air-gapped customer settings."},"check":{"q":"A target claims a \"proprietary model\". How would you test that claim and why does the answer matter\nto the deal?","a":"I would ask for the production architecture and then verify it in traces and billing: which\nmodel calls are made, to which providers, with what weights or adapters. \"Proprietary\" might mean a model\ntrained from scratch, a fine-tune of an open-weight base, or prompts and retrieval around a third-party\nAPI. Each has different value and risk: a fine-tune needs data rights and license checks on the base\nmodel; a wrapper depends on provider pricing and terms, so its moat is workflow, data and distribution. I\nwould then measure how much better it is than a strong off-the-shelf baseline on our own held-out data.\nThe answer changes the valuation, not necessarily the decision."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the single most informative test in AI technical due diligence?","options":["Reviewing the target's architecture diagram","Running the system on a held-out sample the acquirer provides, scored by the acquirer's criteria","Counting the number of models the target has trained","Reading the target's published benchmark results"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Independent testing on data the target did not choose removes curation and contamination effects. A gap between their number and yours directly measures how far the headline claim transfers."},{"q":"Why can a healthy gross margin at current usage be misleading for an AI product?","options":["Gross margin excludes all infrastructure costs by definition","AI products always have negative margins at scale","Inference cost grows with tokens per task and usage, and may rest on temporary provider discounts","Revenue is always recognised later for AI products"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Features like longer context or multi-step agents multiply tokens per task, and discounts can lapse. Unless pricing passes those costs through, margin falls as usage grows."},{"q":"A target's eval set was partly used to tune its prompts. What symptom would you expect on fresh data?","options":["Higher accuracy than reported, because prompts generalise","Lower accuracy than reported, since the reported score partly reflects fitting to those examples","Identical accuracy, because prompt tuning does not affect evaluation","Random accuracy, because prompts are model-specific"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Iterating on prompts against an eval set fits the system to those examples, just like training on test data. The reported score overstates performance on genuinely new inputs."}]},{"key":"strategy/stakeholder-alignment","sources":[],"why":"An AI project touches more parties than a typical feature: a business owner who wants a cost or speed\ngain, the operators whose workflow changes, legal and compliance who own regulatory risk, security who\nowns data exposure, IT or the platform team who must run it, finance who funds the inference bill, and\nsometimes works councils or unions. Each has a different definition of success, and several have an\neffective veto.\n\nThe naive approach is to align on the vision (\"AI-assisted claims triage\") and start building, expecting\ndetails to settle later. It fails because the disagreements are not about the vision, they are about the\nnumbers. The operations lead wants most claims handled automatically; compliance wants zero wrongful\ndenials; finance wants a cost per claim below today's. With a probabilistic system these goals trade off\ndirectly against each other, and if the trade-off is not settled up front it gets settled at launch,\nusually by a late veto. Projects then die in \"pilot purgatory\": the demo impressed, the eval looks good to\nengineering, but there was never an agreed bar, so nobody can say yes. A related failure is the implicit\nstandard of perfection: without an agreed baseline, any visible AI error is judged against an imagined\nerror-free human process rather than the real one.","how":"### 1. Map who matters and what each can do\nList stakeholders with their stake (what they gain or lose), their power (fund, approve, veto, adopt,\nblock quietly) and their main fear. Pay most attention to those who can veto late: legal, security, data\nprotection, and the frontline users whose non-adoption is a silent veto. Engaging them first is cheaper\n*because* objections raised before design change a requirement; raised after build, they change a plan.\n\n### 2. Elicit success criteria and red lines separately\nFor each stakeholder ask what outcome they need (a metric) and what they cannot accept (a constraint).\nConstraints are hard limits (\"no customer data leaves the EU\", \"no automated denial without human\nreview\"); outcomes are things to optimise. Keeping them separate prevents a constraint being traded away\nin a negotiation over an outcome.\n\n### 3. Measure the baseline\nMeasure the current process on the same metrics: human error rate, cycle time, cost per item. This\nreplaces \"AI must never be wrong\" with \"AI plus review must beat today's process by an agreed margin\",\nwhich is a standard a system can actually meet. Measure it on the *same items* the AI will handle,\n*because* a system that automates only its confident cases is taking the easier slice of the work: humans\nare usually more accurate on that slice than their overall rate suggests, so comparing the AI's error on\nits slice with the humans' error on everything flatters the AI. Historical decisions on the labelled eval\nset usually give you this for free.\n\n### 4. Agree the operating point with evidence\nRun an offline eval on representative data and show the trade-off curve: as the confidence threshold for\nautomation rises, fewer items are automated and fewer are wrong. Stakeholders then choose a point on a\nreal curve instead of arguing in the abstract. This is the AI-specific heart of alignment.\n\n### 5. Pre-wire before the decision meeting\nWalk each veto holder through the evidence one-to-one before the group meeting, *because* an objection\nraised privately can be met with a design change, while one raised for the first time in front of peers\ntends to harden into a position. The group meeting should then ratify a choice everyone has already seen,\nwith any remaining disagreement named and escalated to the agreed decision owner rather than re-argued.\n\n### 6. Write it down in one page\nGoal, primary metric with baseline and target, hard constraints, the operating point and why, failure\nhandling (fallback to humans, escalation path, rollback trigger), decision rights (who approves launch,\nwho can pause it; a RACI or DACI model works), and review cadence. A written artefact matters *because*\nmemories of verbal agreements diverge within weeks.\n\n### 7. Re-align at each gate\nPresent results against the one-pager at each roadmap gate. If results move the trade-off (accuracy is\nlower than hoped, costs are higher), re-open the decision explicitly rather than drifting.","example":"A worked scenario: claims triage at an insurer, three stakeholders with conflicting goals. Figures are\nillustrative.\n\n- **Operations VP:** wants at least 50% of claims routed automatically.\n- **Compliance:** red line, wrong-queue errors on auto-routed claims must be no worse than today's manual\n  error rate, which is 4% across all claims.\n- **Finance:** cost per claim below today's $3.10.\n\nThe team runs the classifier on 1,000 labelled historical claims and tabulates by confidence threshold:\n\n| Threshold | Share auto-routed | Error rate on auto-routed | Blended cost per claim |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 0.70 | 78% | 6.5% | $1.20 |\n| 0.85 | 61% | 3.2% | $1.75 |\n| 0.95 | 38% | 1.1% | $2.30 |\n\nAt 0.70, ops gets its volume but the compliance red line is breached. At 0.95, compliance is comfortable\nbut ops is well short of 50%. At 0.85 all three look satisfied: 61% automated, 3.2% error, cost well under\n$3.10. Two checks make the margin thinner than it looks. First, the fair baseline: the labelled claims\ncarry their original human routing, and on the same 610 claims the model would automate, humans erred\n3.5% of the time, not 4% (on the 390 harder claims they erred about 4.8%; 0.61 × 3.5% + 0.39 × 4.8% ≈ 4.0%).\nSo the model is roughly at parity on its slice, and the whole process improves only from about 4.0% to\n0.61 × 3.2% + 0.39 × 4.8% ≈ 3.8%. Second, noise: 3.2% of 610 is roughly 20 errors, and its 95% interval\nruns to about 4.6%. Compliance agrees parity is acceptable, since the red line was \"no worse\". The\none-pager therefore records 0.85 as a provisional operating point, a rollback trigger if the production error rate\nover a rolling audited sample (large enough, a few hundred claims or more, that noise alone does not trip\nit) exceeds 4%, compliance as the owner of that trigger, and a review after a month of production data. The argument that would have happened at\nlaunch happened in a one-hour meeting with a table on the screen.","tradeoffs":["Up-front alignment prevents late vetoes, but delays the start; for a reversible internal experiment, a lighter agreement on the stop condition is enough.","Involving many stakeholders surfaces constraints early, but slows decisions; give advisory voice broadly and decision rights narrowly.","Writing explicit numeric bars makes go/no-go decisions clear, but commits you before the data is fully understood; set them as provisional and re-open at each gate with evidence.","Showing the full error trade-off builds trust with risk owners, but exposes failure rates some sponsors would rather not see; hiding them only moves the discovery to launch, where it costs more."],"pitfalls":["Aligning on vision instead of numbers; the symptom is a successful pilot that nobody can approve because nobody agreed what \"good enough\" meant.","Engaging legal, security or data protection only before launch; the symptom is a late veto or a redesign around a constraint that was knowable on day one.","No baseline for the human process, so any visible AI error is compared with perfection and the project stalls on anecdotes.","Treating frontline users as recipients rather than stakeholders; adoption stays low while every dashboard looks green, a silent veto.","Comparing the AI's error on the cases it chose to automate with the humans' error on all cases; the launch clears the bar, and a later audit shows the automated slice is worse than humans were on those same, easier claims.","Decision rights left implicit; when production metrics wobble, two executives give contradictory instructions and the team freezes."],"when":"Do this before significant build work on any AI system that changes a workflow, touches regulated data,\nor will need sign-off from people outside the building team, which is most of them. Revisit at each\nroadmap gate and whenever evidence moves the trade-off. Skip the heavy version for internal experiments\nthat are cheap to stop. Measure alignment by whether a launch decision can be made from the one-pager and\nthe latest eval without a new negotiation. In interviews, describe turning conflicting goals into a\nchoice on a measured trade-off curve.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the threshold trade-off table and the baseline measurement that let stakeholders choose an operating point with evidence.","architect":"Translates stakeholders' red lines into hard design constraints (residency, human review gates, audit trails) and owns the decision-rights section.","fde":"Runs the alignment meetings inside the customer, identifies the quiet vetoes early, and keeps the one-pager current as the deployment evolves."},"check":{"q":"The business sponsor says the AI must be \"100% accurate\" before launch. How do you move the\nconversation forward?","a":"I would not argue with the goal; I would make the comparison concrete. First measure the current\nprocess: how often human handlers make the same kind of error, and what that costs. Then show the eval\nresults as a trade-off: at a given confidence threshold, what share of items the system handles, its error\nrate on those, and what happens to the rest (human review). The question becomes \"which operating point\nbeats today's process, with which safeguards\", plus a rollback trigger if production errors exceed the\nbaseline. Most sponsors accept a measured improvement with a safety net once they see the human error\nrate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why separate stakeholders' hard constraints from their outcome goals?","options":["Constraints are only relevant to legal teams","So a red line cannot be traded away during negotiation over metrics that are meant to be optimised","Outcomes are always less important than constraints","Constraints are easier to measure than outcomes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Outcomes such as automation rate are legitimately traded against each other; constraints such as data residency are not. Mixing them invites treating a red line as one more weight in the balance."},{"q":"What makes alignment on AI systems harder than on deterministic features?","options":["AI systems need more stakeholders by regulation","Stakeholders cannot understand model architectures","Goals like automation rate and error rate trade off directly, so an operating point must be chosen","AI systems cannot be measured before deployment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A probabilistic system is right only part of the time, so \"does it work\" depends on the chosen threshold. Stakeholders with different goals must agree on a point on that trade-off curve."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of measuring the human baseline before deployment?","options":["To show that the human team should be reduced","To train the model on human mistakes","To replace an implicit perfection standard with a realistic bar the system can be judged against","To satisfy a requirement of most model providers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without a baseline, every AI error is compared with an imagined error-free process. Measuring today's error rate and cost gives an agreed, achievable bar for the go/no-go decision."}]}]});
